            {
              "feedback": {
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Incorrect. The rate law for an overall reaction is the same as the rate law for the rate-determining (or rate-limiting) step of the reaction, and the rate law for an elementary reaction is expressed using the rate constant ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "k"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "), the concentration of the reactant(s), and the order of the reaction (exponent) for each reactant. However, the rate law for an overall reaction cannot include intermediate species. If the rate-determining step includes an intermediate, then use the rate laws from the other elementary reactions to derive an equation that shows the intermediate in terms of one or more reactants. Then substitute the intermediate in the overall rate law with its equivalent in terms of one or more reactants. "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2xbkj9z7ljnrh2y",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
